    "safety": {
      "cautions": [
        "Stop if you feel sharp, radiating or nerve-type pain.",
        "Muscle glow colours and activation curves are illustrative, not measured EMG.",
        "Choose a dumbbell load you can control for every rep; the load is user-chosen.",
        "Low-back pain, disc problems or flexion intolerance: start with a McGill-style curl-up, dead bug or plank; partial curl-ups gave the best abdominal challenge for the least spine compression in one model study (Axler and McGill 1997).",
        "Shoulder pain or limited overhead mobility: hold the dumbbell on the chest instead.",
        "Never let the dumbbell pass over the face with bent, tired arms; end the set early.",
        "Pregnancy and postpartum (diastasis recti): get individual guidance before dynamic trunk-flexion work.",
        "Osteoporosis or vertebral fracture: avoid repeated loaded spinal flexion and twisting.",
        "The athlete mesh rests on the mat; the rig collision shapes are thicker than the mesh at the back and buttocks, so floorTolerance records how far those proxy shapes sink below the mat surface."
      ],
      "modify": [
        "Dumbbell held on the chest (shorter lever).",
        "Lighter dumbbell or a plate."
      ],
      "regressions": [
        "Crunch with the hands overhead (no weight).",
        "Crunch with the dumbbell on the chest."
      ],
      "progressions": [
        "Heavier dumbbell.",
        "Slow 3 s lowering with a 2 s hold."
      ]
    },
    "muscleNotes": {
      "core": "Rectus abdominis curls the trunk against the dumbbell held at the end of straight arms. Loading the curl-up by moving the arms raised activity in all abdominal muscles studied (Crommert 2021); a plain crunch is about 53% upper and 39% lower rectus MVIC (Escamilla 2016), so this version sits well above it.",
      "obliques_r": "Assist trunk flexion and rise with the load (Crommert 2021).",
      "obliques_l": "Assist trunk flexion and rise with the load (Crommert 2021).",
      "ant_deltoid_r": "Holds the loaded arms up beside the ears (clinical opinion).",
      "ant_deltoid_l": "Holds the loaded arms up beside the ears (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_r": "Lengthened under load overhead; they steady the arms and the rib cage (clinical opinion).",
      "lats_l": "Lengthened under load overhead; they steady the arms and the rib cage (clinical opinion).",
      "triceps_r": "Keep the elbows straight under the dumbbell (clinical opinion).",
      "triceps_l": "Keep the elbows straight under the dumbbell (clinical opinion).",
      "hip_flexors_r": "Only steady the bent legs; rectus femoris is about 6% MVIC in a crunch (Escamilla 2016).",
      "hip_flexors_l": "Only steady the bent legs; rectus femoris is about 6% MVIC in a crunch (Escamilla 2016)."
    }
